{
  "case": "Rust sparse warm update on a live graph",
  "protocol": "One maintained sparse SUM workload on main 644f8f15. Compare the same release executable and compiled graph shape with cumulative snapshot output (A) and first-touch changelog output (B). Reuse the existing fixed sparse.arrow fixture, SHA-256 and per-batch reconstruction oracle. 10,000 seeded groups; twenty 100-row updates, 80 changed groups each. No time for seed, graph start, IPC import, independent full-value validation, EOF or shutdown inside the warm update observations. Start immediately before sending a ready Batch to the bounded input (capacity one), stop on receipt of its sole output from the ordinary sink. Retain every output through clean EOF. Only one update may be outstanding. Run one single-CPU fresh process per arm per pair (one warmup per arm per round, 2 rounds of 10 adjacent alternating-order pairs). Capture all 20 intervals per process, each process median is the unit for paired sign intervals; separately describe pooled P50/P95. One report per mode; snapshot and changelog schemas and output sizes differ by design, reconstructed states must match for all 21. Apply the maintained >=5% paired regression rule. Proposed stage target B public Rust graph warm update P50 <=250 us. Single-core full 21-batch target remains 10ms (different clock). Fixed budget 240 seconds including copying the fixture, preflight, warmups, process startup, all validation and cleanup. Release compilation estimated 15\u201330 minutes separately. Python timings require their own public API run.\n",
